LM.SEARCH_INDEX = {"version":1,"records":[{"id":"page:a1-verbs-gustar","group":"pages","title":"gustar and similar verbs","headings":["How it works","When to use it","Examples","Good to know","Common mistakes","Practice","Answers"],"summary":"Say what you like and don't like. In Spanish the thing you like is the subject, not you.","text":"In English you say \"I like coffee\", and I is the subject. Spanish turns the sentence around: Me gusta el café is, word for word, \"Coffee pleases me\". The thing you like is the subject; you are the person it pleases. That is why you only need two forms of the verb: gusta and gustan. How it works (a + person) + pronoun + gusta / gustan + what you like Who likes it Pronoun One thing, or an action Several things (a mí) me gusta gustan (a ti) te gusta gustan (a él, a ella, a usted) le gusta gustan (a nosotros, a nosotras) nos gusta gustan (a vosotros, a vosotras) (Spain) os gusta gustan (a ellos, a ellas, a ustedes) les gusta gustan gusta + singular noun with its article: Me gusta el café. gusta + infinitive, also with two or more infinitives: Me gusta bailar. Me gusta cantar y bailar. gustan + plural noun: Me gustan los gatos. Negative: no goes before the pronoun: No me gusta el té. Questions keep the same word order: ¿Te gusta el fútbol? When to use it Say what you like or don't like. Me gusta la música. (I like music.) No me gustan los lunes. (I don't like Mondays.) Ask someone. To a friend: ¿Te gusta cocinar? Formal: ¿Le gusta la habitación? Say how much. Me gusta mucho el cine. (I like movies a lot.) No me gusta nada el frío. (I don't like the cold at all.) Me encanta means \"I love\": Me encanta esta ciudad. Make clear who. Le can mean he, she or you (formal), so add a + the person: A María le gusta el cine. A mí no. Agree or disagree. —Me gusta el café. —A mí también. (Me too.) —No me gusta el té. —A mí tampoco. (Me neither.) Examples Spanish English Me gusta el chocolate. I like chocolate. ¿Te gustan los perros? Do you like dogs? No nos gusta trabajar los sábados. We don't like working on Saturdays. A Carlos le gusta mucho viajar. Carlos really likes traveling. A mis padres les gustan las películas antiguas. My parents like old movies. Me encanta esta ciudad. I love this city. ¿Os gusta la comida mexicana? (Spain) Do you all like Mexican food? —No me gusta el frío. —A mí tampoco. \"I don't like the cold.\" \"Me neither.\" Good to know The same pattern works for encantar (to love), interesar (to interest) and doler (to hurt): Me interesan los idiomas. Me duele la cabeza. Me encanta is already strong. Do not add mucho. About people, Me gustas means \"I am attracted to you\". To say you like someone as a person, use Me cae bien: Tu hermano me cae muy bien. Common mistakes ✗ Yo gusto el café. → ✓ Me gusta el café. — you are not the subject; the coffee is. ✗ Me gusta los gatos. → ✓ Me gustan los gatos. — the verb agrees with the thing you like. ✗ Me gusta café. → ✓ Me gusta el café. — things you like in general need the article. ✗ María le gusta bailar. → ✓ A María le gusta bailar. — the person needs a. ✗ Me gusto bailar. → ✓ Me gusta bailar. — me gusto means \"I like myself\". Practice Complete with the pronoun and gusta or gustan. (a mí) la paella. (a ti) ¿ los deportes? A Ana leer. (a nosotros) No las verduras. A mis amigos cantar y bailar. Translate into Spanish. I don't like coffee. Do you like the hotel? (formal) \"I love dogs.\" \"Me too.\" Answers Me gusta Te gustan le gusta nos gustan les gusta No me gusta el café. ¿Le gusta el hotel? —Me encantan los perros. —A mí también.","level":"A1","category":"Verbs: tenses and moods","route":"#/a1/verbs-gustar"},{"id":"review","group":"exercises","title":"Flashcards and mistakes","headings":[],"summary":"One review queue: deck cards, My mistakes and weak spots from exercises.","text":"flashcards review decks mistakes weak spots","level":null,"category":"Flashcards","route":"#/review"},{"id":"review:words","group":"exercises","title":"My words","headings":[],"summary":"Words and phrases you selected in texts, with their sentence and meaning.","text":"my words personal vocabulary flashcards","level":null,"category":"Flashcards","route":"#/review/words"},{"id":"deck:vocab-a1-food-shopping","group":"exercises","title":"Food, drink and shopping (flashcards)","headings":[],"summary":"Sample of 8 cards. The full deck has 30–40 cards for the topic a1-vocabulary-food-shopping.","text":"flashcards deck Food, drink and shopping A1","level":null,"category":"Flashcards","route":"#/review/decks/deck/vocab-a1-food-shopping"},{"id":"set:a1-verbs-gustar-1","group":"exercises","title":"gustar: check yourself","headings":[],"summary":"10 tasks: choose the form, fill the gaps, build sentences and translate.","text":"Topic: gustar and similar verbs","level":"A1","category":"Exercise set","route":"#/practice/set/a1-verbs-gustar-1"},{"id":"set:a1-verbs-gustar-2","group":"exercises","title":"gustar: write it yourself","headings":[],"summary":"10 tasks: type the missing word, rewrite sentences, write what you hear and build a question.","text":"Topic: gustar and similar verbs","level":"A1","category":"Exercise set","route":"#/practice/set/a1-verbs-gustar-2"},{"id":"set:a1-verbs-gustar-3","group":"exercises","title":"gustar: agree, disagree and say how much","headings":[],"summary":"10 tasks: listen and write, rewrite, find the mistake, match reactions and fill a table with encantar.","text":"Topic: gustar and similar verbs","level":"A1","category":"Exercise set","route":"#/practice/set/a1-verbs-gustar-3"},{"id":"lab:presente","group":"lab","title":"Presente","headings":["Present"],"summary":"What is true now, what you do regularly, and general facts.","text":"True now Vivo en un apartamento pequeño cerca del centro. I live in a small apartment near downtown. Habits and routines Los sábados juego al tenis con mi hermana. On Saturdays I play tennis with my sister. General facts El sol sale por el este. The sun rises in the east. Happening at the moment ¿Qué haces? —Leo una novela. What are you doing? —I'm reading a novel. Fixed plans and timetables Mañana trabajo hasta las ocho. Tomorrow I'm working until eight. siempre normalmente todos los días los lunes a veces nunca ahora English says “I'm reading” for an action going on now. Spanish can just use the present: Leo una novela. Estoy leyendo is also very common, but you do not need it every time. The ending already shows the person, so the subject pronoun is usually left out: Vivo en Lima. Using yo in every sentence sounds unnatural; keep it for emphasis or contrast. Stem changes do not happen in the nosotros and vosotros forms: queremos, podemos, not *quieremos, *puedemos.","level":"A1","category":"Lab card","route":"#/lab/presente"},{"id":"lab:preterito-indefinido","group":"lab","title":"Pretérito indefinido","headings":["Preterite"],"summary":"Finished actions at a definite time in the past.","text":"One finished action Ayer comí con Lucía. Yesterday I had lunch with Lucía. A chain of actions Llegué a casa, cené y me acosté. I got home, had dinner and went to bed. A closed period of time Viví tres años en Lima. I lived in Lima for three years. ayer anoche el lunes la semana pasada en 2019 hace dos años The English simple past covers both one finished event and a habit: “I ate there yesterday” / “As a kid I ate there every Sunday”. Spanish separates them: comí (once, finished) and comía (habit or background). Present yo and preterite él differ only by the accent: hablo (I speak) / habló (he or she spoke). The preterite yo form is hablé.","level":"A2","category":"Lab card","route":"#/lab/preterito-indefinido"},{"id":"lab:preterito-imperfecto","group":"lab","title":"Pretérito imperfecto","headings":["Imperfect"],"summary":"Descriptions, background and habits in the past.","text":"Habits in the past De niña vivía en un pueblo e iba a la escuela a pie. As a girl I lived in a village and walked to school. Descriptions La casa era vieja y tenía un jardín enorme. The house was old and had a huge garden. Background to an event Hacía frío y llovía cuando llegamos. It was cold and raining when we arrived. Time and how someone felt Eran las once y yo tenía mucho sueño. It was eleven o'clock and I was very sleepy. antes de niño / de niña siempre todos los veranos normalmente mientras cuando era joven English “used to play” and “was playing” are both often jugaba in Spanish. The imperfecto covers habits and actions in progress. The imperfecto does not tell you that an action finished. For one finished event at a definite time, use the indefinido: Ayer vi a Pablo. Ayer veía a Pablo sounds unfinished, as if the story goes on. Watch the accents: hablábamos (only the nosotros form of -ar verbs), but every -er and -ir form has í: comía, vivíamos.","level":"A2","category":"Lab card","route":"#/lab/preterito-imperfecto"},{"id":"lab:ir-a-infinitivo","group":"lab","title":"ir a + infinitivo","headings":["going to + infinitive"],"summary":"Plans, intentions and things that are clearly about to happen.","text":"Plans Este verano vamos a viajar a Perú. This summer we're going to travel to Peru. Intentions El próximo año voy a estudiar más. Next year I'm going to study more. About to happen (you can see it) ¡Cuidado, te vas a caer! Careful, you're going to fall! Let's… (vamos a) Bueno, vamos a empezar. OK, let's start. mañana esta noche este fin de semana el próximo año la semana que viene pronto Do not leave out a: Voy a comer, not *Voy comer. After a comes the infinitive, never a conjugated verb: Vamos a salir, not *Vamos a salimos. English “I'm going to the movies” has no second verb. In Spanish that is ir a + a place: Voy al cine.","level":"A1","category":"Lab card","route":"#/lab/ir-a-infinitivo"},{"id":"lab:ser-estar","group":"lab","title":"ser vs estar","headings":["what someone or something is","where it is and how it is now"],"summary":"Two verbs for “to be”: ser says what something is, estar says where it is and how it is right now.","text":"Identity and jobs Who or what someone is, including their job. Origin Where someone or something comes from. What it is like Lasting characteristics: personality, looks, size, color. Events Where or when an event happens: a party, a class, a concert. Time and dates The time, the day and the date. Location Where a person or a thing is, even when it never moves. (Where an event happens takes ser.) Feelings and states How someone feels or what state something is in at the moment. bien and mal Asking and saying how someone is. *Soy cansado → Estoy cansado. Feelings and states take estar. *Está médico → Es médico. Jobs take ser. *La farmacia es en esta calle → La farmacia está en esta calle. Location takes estar. *Soy 20 años → Tengo 20 años. Age takes tener, not ser or estar.","level":"A1","category":"Contrast card","route":"#/lab/contrast/ser-estar"},{"id":"lab-tree:ser-or-estar","group":"lab","title":"ser or estar?","headings":[],"summary":"Think of the sentence you want to say with “to be”. A few questions lead to the right verb, and sometimes it is neither of the two.","text":"What does “to be” tell us in your sentence? Where a person or a thing is estar Where an event happens: a party, a class, a concert ser Who someone is, their job, or where they are from ser The time, the day or the date ser How old someone is tener What someone or something is like, or how they are  Is it what they are like in general, or how they are at the moment? What they are like in general: personality, looks, size, color ser How they feel or how they are right now: tired, glad, ill, busy estar Just “How are you?” or “I'm fine” estar","level":"A1","category":"Decision tree","route":"#/lab/pick/ser-or-estar"}]};
